# A dropped client object should release its lease

Raised by Carl (2026-08-25): "The RPC client is keeping server-side
values alive, once they go out of scope/destroyed/whatever on the RPC
client it should send an RPC that it's been forgotten by the client so
the server can garbage collect when there are no holders left."

## Confirmed

Probed against a running server: acquire an EvalState, produce 50
Values, drop every client-side reference, gc.collect().

    dropped 50 client objects; 50/50 still live server-side

That is the whole of the current behaviour. `sweep()` reaps a
CONNECTION that has gone silent past the TTL, and nothing else. A
client that pings - which the client does, on a timer, precisely so it
is not swept - keeps every handle it has ever been granted until it
disconnects. A long-running evaluation server (016) would grow without
bound, and the objects behind those handles are Values pinning their
EvalState through the parent graph.

Explicit `release()` and `aclose()` work. They are just not what a
Python caller does: a caller drops a name and expects the resource to
follow.

## The shape of the fix

The server side needs nothing. `release()` already decrements, `_reap()`
already cascades, and the entry survives while any lease or live child
remains. This is entirely about the client noticing.

    weakref.finalize(obj, ...) -> enqueue the handle id
    the flush turns the queue into one rpc

Four hazards, none of them optional:

1. **Aliasing.** `client.proxy(cls, hid)` can build two Python objects
   for one handle - test_lifecycle does it seven times deliberately.
   Per-object finalizers would then have the first drop release a
   handle the second object still uses. The client needs a refcount
   per handle id, releasing only at zero. In normal traffic this never
   arises (`put()` mints a fresh id per return, so one handle has one
   object), but `proxy()` is public API.

2. **Finalizers cannot await.** A finalize callback runs on whichever
   thread dropped the last reference, possibly during interpreter
   shutdown. It can only enqueue. The flush belongs on the existing
   ping loop, which already runs on the event loop on a timer, plus
   opportunistically before another rpc.

3. **Resurrection between enqueue and flush.** A handle queued for
   release, then `proxy()`d again before the flush, must not be
   released. Check the refcount is still zero at flush time, not at
   enqueue time.

4. **Double release.** The object may already have been `aclose()`d,
   which blanks `handle_id`. The finalizer must be cancelled there, or
   the flush must skip a blanked id.

Batching wants a `ReleaseMany` on Session taking repeated Handle -
one rpc per flush rather than one per dropped object. That is a schema
change in `_add_session`.

## What stays

The TTL sweep remains the backstop and must: finalizers do not run at
interpreter shutdown reliably, a reference cycle may never collect, and
a killed client sends nothing at all. Connection death releasing
everything is the guarantee; this is the optimisation that keeps a
live connection from growing.

Escrow is unaffected - detached leases are unowned by design.

## Watch out for

test_lifecycle forges proxies from raw handle ids and then asserts
exact lease counts. Adding finalizers changes what those objects do
when they go out of scope, so each of those seven sites needs
rechecking rather than assuming it still means what it meant.

## Done 2026-08-25

All four hazards handled, each with a test that fails when the guard
is removed. Verified by perturbation: dropping the per-handle refcount
makes the aliasing check fail on the twin's first call, with the
handle already released.

    before: dropped 50 client objects; 50/50 still live server-side
    after:  0/50

The flush lives on the ping loop. `flush_dropped()` is public for a
caller that wants it now, and returns how many leases went back.

### Not done, deliberately

Piggybacking. Dropped ids could ride on the next request the client
already makes, instead of a separate rpc. That removes the flush's
round trip entirely, at the cost of putting lifecycle state in every
request message - so it is a protocol change, not a client change.
Worth it only if the extra rpc ever shows up in a measurement.

Responsiveness is bounded by the ping interval (ttl/4, capped at 15s).
A caller that needs a handle gone sooner closes it explicitly, which
is unchanged and immediate.
